ted (evaluating quality of claims, not investigating truth)"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"Measures evidential strength rather than verified accuracy"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"Assesses qualities inherent to the narrative that make veridical perception claims more or less compelling"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"Can be applied to any first-person account without requiring researcher access to witnesses or medical records"}]]}],["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold mt-6","children":"What Strengthens the Evidence for a Veridical Perception Claim?"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"A veridical perception claim carries greater evidential strength when:"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":"The medical state should have precluded any perception"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"Ordinary sensory access to the information was physically impossible"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"The perceived details are specific rather than vague"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"The information could not have been known, guessed, or inferred"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"The experiencer actively sought to verify the perception"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"Perceptions that were verified carry more weight than unverified claims"}],["$","li",null,{"children":"The perception was reported before the experiencer could have learned it was accurate"}]]}]]}],["$","hr",null,{"className":"border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-800"}],["$","section",null,{"className":"space-y-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-semibold","children":"Scale Criteria"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold","children":"Criterion 1: Medical State Severity During Perception"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"italic","children":"What was the reported medical/physical state during which the veridical perceptions occurred?"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"This criterion assesses how compromised brain function reportedly was during the experience. The more severe the medical crisis, the greater the evidential strength of any accurate perception, as conventional neuroscience would predict no perception should be possible."}],"$L3","$L4"]}],"$L5","$L6","$L7","$L8","$L9","$La"]}],"$Lb","$Lc","$Ld","$Le"]}]}],null,"$Lf"]}],"loading":null,"isPartial":false}
